# OTRv4+ shipped I2P name aliases.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE IS FOR
# ---------------------
# `.i2p` names are not DNS.  A router resolves only what is in its own
# address book, and a server that has just been set up is in nobody's.  So a
# fresh install would have to paste a 52-character b32 to connect at all.
#
# This file travels with the source.  `git pull` updates it like any other
# code, which is also how a server move reaches people: ship a new line here,
# and a user running the same command as always connects to the new address.
#
# PRECEDENCE
# ----------
# ~/.otrv4plus/i2p_hosts is the user's own file and an update NEVER edits it.
#   * a hand-written line there always wins over this file;
#   * a line this client learned by itself (marked "# learned") yields to a
#     changed default here, with a notice -- otherwise the first successful
#     connection would pin an address forever and moving a server would
#     strand everyone who had ever connected.
#
# WHAT AN ALIAS IS NOT
# --------------------
# It is a convenience, not an authority.  Nothing here is authenticated and
# nothing here proves who answers.  It does not need to be: the server is not
# a trust anchor in this design.  The DAKE authenticates the peer and TOFU
# pins their identity key end to end, so a wrong address costs a failed
# connection, not a readable conversation.  If you do not want to trust this
# file at all, delete its contents -- everything still works with an explicit
# --server.
#
# Format:  name.i2p = <52 chars>.b32.i2p
#
xmpp-elite.i2p = hq4t24b7vkllfbk55e5xfocqhfi7hxprwc47zyuilbg6wgzikidq.b32.i2p

# Retired destinations.  A b32 is NEVER silently redirected -- an address
# given in full is used as given -- but if it is listed here the client can
# say that it moved and name the current form.
#
# Format:  retired <old 52 chars>.b32.i2p <name it moved to>
